you got Belcher versus Belcher,

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:13.920
so two siblings going against each other.

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:15.990
- Yeah, is that an ethical violation?

00:03:15.990 --> 00:03:17.670
- Well, under ABA rules,

00:03:17.670 --> 00:03:20.160
you have to get consent from your clients,

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:21.600
because there is a
little bit of a concern.

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:23.760
If you know the other party's lawyer,

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:26.070
maybe you're gonna give
some information away,

00:03:26.070 --> 00:03:27.450
if you have some relationship.

00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:30.030
- And we can't forget the
most important role of all,

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:31.500
Tina, the juror.

00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:33.750
- With great jury comes
great responsibility.

00:03:33.750 --> 00:03:36.420
- Well, you definitely
can't have a family member

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:38.100
on the jury.
- Yes, I mean,

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:40.350
so, I mean, we talk about
peremptory challenges

00:03:40.350 --> 00:03:42.840
versus challenges for
cause a lot in voir dire.

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:44.220
I have to imagine that would be

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:46.127
an automatic for cause challenge.

00:03:46.127 --> 00:03:47.610
- I wish I could sit on the jury

00:03:47.610 --> 00:03:50.400
and zone out while we do a
mock trial of Snow White.

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:53.040
Whoa, do you think the Evil Queen

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:55.440
gave Snow White a poison apple?

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:56.273
- Yes.

00:03:56.273 --> 00:03:58.680
- That's for a jury of our
peers to decide, but probably.

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:00.240
- It's just so baby-ish.

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:03.060
- In high school, I also
participated in a mock trial

00:04:03.060 --> 00:04:04.320
in my English class, I think,

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:06.240
which is what they're doing here.

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:07.710
I think it was Antigone,

00:04:07.710 --> 00:04:09.453
so we read the Greek play.

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:11.460
That doesn't make any sense,

00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:15.570
because Antigone commits suicide,

00:04:15.570 --> 00:04:18.320
so I don't think we would've
put her on trial for that.

00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:23.190
King Creon said, oh, that's what it was,

00:04:23.190 --> 00:04:27.360
don't bury the body of
Poluneices, and she did.

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:30.120
This is a really esoteric issue

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:31.020
that we're dealing with here.

00:04:31.020 --> 00:04:33.570
- How many viewers out
there know this story?

00:04:33.570 --> 00:04:34.530
- Yes.

00:04:34.530 --> 00:04:36.990
Thank you for tuning
into legal antiquities.

00:04:36.990 --> 00:04:39.180
We're gonna be covering
all of Sophocles' plays.

00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:41.100
It's a completely new channel.

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:43.219
- Legal BC, Legal You'll Be Seeing.

00:04:43.219 --> 00:04:44.520
- Yeah, now that we have one viewer left,

00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:47.370
let's, I guess, get on
with "Bob's Burgers".

00:04:47.370 --> 00:04:50.040
- Okay, everyone, Judge
Conklin is just outside

00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:51.210
and he's ready to begin.

00:04:51.210 --> 00:04:53.700
He's retired, but today
he's gonna hear our case

00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:55.350
and guide us through the process.

00:04:55.350 --> 00:04:57.480
Bailiff Zeke, why don't
you get us started?

00:04:57.480 --> 00:04:59.100
- Hear ye, hear ye.

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:02.704
The court of the Honorable
Judge Conklin is now in session.

00:05:02.704 --> 00:05:04.552
- I like how he has a cutoff.

00:05:04.552 --> 00:05:05.385
- Yeah.

00:05:05.385 --> 00:05:06.218
- Maybe you should call me

00:05:06.218 --> 00:05:07.748
the somewhat respectable Judge Conklin.

00:05:07.748 --> 00:05:10.350
- (laughs) Oh, gah, he's funny.

00:05:10.350 --> 00:05:13.200
- Ooh, the somewhat wet
and messy Judge Conklin.

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:15.837
I had little accident,
just water, not pee.

00:05:15.837 --> 00:05:18.450
- (laughs) Oh, god, this
guy has got no filter.

00:05:18.450 --> 00:05:19.283
Be seated.

00:05:19.283 --> 00:05:22.920
- So what's your policy on
making jokes in the courtroom?

00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:24.630
- Oh, definitely wouldn't do it.

00:05:24.630 --> 00:05:25.463
- Never at all?

00:05:25.463 --> 00:05:28.530
- I mean, yeah, I try
to stray away from it.

00:05:28.530 --> 00:05:30.720
Obviously, you can
understand how the judge is,

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:33.450
depending on, if it's your
first time seeing the judge,

00:05:33.450 --> 00:05:35.610
I always take the more
conservative approach.

00:05:35.610 --> 00:05:38.280
You know, if the other
side is doing silly stuff

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:40.260
or things that are making the judge angry,

00:05:40.260 --> 00:05:41.610
let them go off on.

00:05:41.610 --> 00:05:42.840
- Yeah, of course, yeah.

00:05:42.840 --> 00:05:45.270
And I think we can both
agree, the policy is that,

00:05:45.270 --> 00:05:47.397
when the judge makes the
joke, you laugh at the joke.

00:05:47.397 --> 00:05:48.990
- [Mike] Oh, you gotta laugh really hard.

00:05:48.990 --> 00:05:51.180
Like it's the funniest
joke you've ever heard.

00:05:51.180 --> 00:05:53.310
- Never in my life have
I heard anything funnier

00:05:53.310 --> 00:05:55.943
than whatever the joke.
- It is the best joke.

00:05:55.943 --> 00:05:57.030
Judge should be a standup comedian.

00:05:57.030 --> 00:05:59.610
- Your Honor, ladies and
gentlemen of the jury,

00:05:59.610 --> 00:06:00.570
here are the facts.

00:06:00.570 --> 00:06:02.610
The Evil Queen was jealous of my client,

00:06:02.610 --> 00:06:05.010
the gorgeous but a little sun
wouldn't kill her Snow White,

00:06:05.010 --> 00:06:06.300
and gave her a poison apple,

00:06:06.300 --> 00:06:08.190
sending her into a sleeping death.

00:06:08.190 --> 00:06:10.020
To sum it up, not cool,

00:06:10.020 --> 00:06:13.050
so do your thing and
return a verdict of guilty.

00:06:13.050 --> 00:06:14.910
- Okay.
- That's pretty argumentative

00:06:14.910 --> 00:06:16.650
for an opening statement.
- Oh, yeah.

00:06:16.650 --> 00:06:19.200
Yeah, you can't have
argumentative portions

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:21.300
in your opening statements,

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:22.517
and not cool, I mean, yeah,

00:06:22.517 --> 00:06:24.300
that's pretty much an
argument right there,

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:25.133
if you asked me.

00:06:25.133 --> 00:06:26.160
- Pretty, pretty light on facts.

00:06:26.160 --> 00:06:29.550
You're supposed to tell the
jury what is going to come out.

00:06:29.550 --> 00:06:31.200
You're supposed to proffer

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:32.730
what the witnesses are going to say,

00:06:32.730 --> 00:06:33.690
and maybe, at the end,

00:06:33.690 --> 00:06:35.790
you might say and
because of this evidence,

00:06:35.790 --> 00:06:38.490
we will ask you to return
a verdict of guilty

00:06:38.490 --> 00:06:39.630
at the end of the trial.

00:06:39.630 --> 00:06:41.700
- We save the arguments for closing,

00:06:41.700 --> 00:06:44.250
after the jury has seen all the evidence,

00:06:44.250 --> 00:06:46.920
and then you can argue why that
evidence proves your point.

00:06:46.920 --> 00:06:49.740
- Yeah, it's opening statements,
not opening arguments.

00:06:49.740 --> 00:06:50.728
- Exactly.

00:06:50.728 --> 00:06:53.644
- Okay, counterpoint, she's not guilty.

00:06:53.644 --> 00:06:54.477
Thank you.

00:06:54.477 --> 00:06:55.333
- Okay, well,

00:06:55.333 --> 00:06:57.510
that was even worse than
the first one. (laughs)

00:06:57.510 --> 00:06:58.620
- Yeah, that was pretty bad.

00:06:58.620 --> 00:06:59.610
I mean, at that point,

00:06:59.610 --> 00:07:01.860
one thing that most people
don't realize that you can

00:07:01.860 --> 00:07:04.410
and often should do is just
reserve your opening statement

00:07:04.410 --> 00:07:07.020
until after the
prosecution's case in chief,

00:07:07.020 --> 00:07:08.700
so that you can craft
your opening statement

00:07:08.700 --> 00:07:12.120
to respond to their points
and craft your entire case.

00:07:12.120 --> 00:07:13.740
- You know, sometimes it's better to know

00:07:13.740 --> 00:07:15.240
what you're going against,

00:07:15.240 --> 00:07:19.200
especially 'cause the jury
usually forgets what you say.

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:21.690
By the time you go and
have to present your case,

00:07:21.690 --> 00:07:22.800
they've already forgotten your opening.

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:26.370
- Oh, yeah, the recency
effect is absolutely real.

00:07:26.370 --> 00:07:28.440
No one will remember
what the prosecution said

00:07:28.440 --> 00:07:29.820
days and days ago,

00:07:29.820 --> 00:07:31.740
so to be able to give an opening statement

00:07:31.740 --> 00:07:33.900
much later in the case
can be a huge advantage.

00:07:33.900 --> 00:07:35.800
- Yeah, what was that?

00:07:35.800 --> 00:07:36.729
I'm kidding.

00:07:36.729 --> 00:07:39.810
(both laugh)

00:07:39.810 --> 00:07:41.430
- I'm a little slow today.

00:07:41.430 --> 00:07:42.810
- What am I supposed to do?

00:07:42.810 --> 00:07:45.540
I mean, her first name is Evil.

00:07:45.540 --> 00:07:47.910
So you're a charming prince, huh?

00:07:47.910 --> 00:07:49.170
- Yes, I am.

00:07:49.170 --> 00:07:52.759
- And what kind of charmer
goes around kissing dead girls?

00:07:52.759 --> 00:07:53.790
- I mean, presumably,

00:07:53.790 --> 00:07:55.140
the prosecution had to call the witness,

00:07:55.140 --> 00:07:57.180
and then she's being-
- She's crossing.

00:07:57.180 --> 00:07:59.340
- She's cross-examining
this witness, right?

00:07:59.340 --> 00:08:01.740
Yeah, so she's allowed
to ask leading questions.

00:08:01.740 --> 00:08:03.270
- So leading questions are allowed

00:08:03.270 --> 00:08:06.840
when you're cross examining
a adverse witness,

00:08:06.840 --> 00:08:09.840
which this person would be,
'cause, like you were saying,

00:08:09.840 --> 00:08:13.440
prosecution probably brought
them up as a direct witness.

00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:16.290
But when it's the other
side, they can't do it,

00:08:16.290 --> 00:08:19.380
because it's almost
like spoon feeding them.

00:08:19.380 --> 00:08:21.990
- And one trick to get around that is,

00:08:21.990 --> 00:08:24.390
if you're directing a witness
and it's your witness,

00:08:24.390 --> 00:08:25.590
basically, all you have to do is say,

00:08:25.590 --> 00:08:26.853
and what happened next?

00:08:27.720 --> 00:08:28.950
If you're ever stuck, just ask,

00:08:28.950 --> 00:08:30.360
and what happened after that?

00:08:30.360 --> 00:08:31.470
- Get off my back.

00:08:31.470 --> 00:08:33.000
- How many corpses did you kiss

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:35.880
before you found your Snow White, Romeo?

00:08:35.880 --> 00:08:36.713
- None.

00:08:36.713 --> 00:08:37.546
- How many?

00:08:37.546 --> 00:08:38.379
- One.
- How many?

00:08:38.379 --> 00:08:39.212
- Four.
- How many?

00:08:39.212 --> 00:08:40.045
- I don't know!

00:08:40.045 --> 00:08:41.790
- Definitely an objection in there.

00:08:41.790 --> 00:08:45.960
- Is the fact that Prince
Charming kissed a corpse

00:08:45.960 --> 00:08:49.620
and arguably created all
kinds of criminal liability,

00:08:49.620 --> 00:08:52.530
is that relevant to what he's
being questioned on here?

00:08:52.530 --> 00:08:54.600
It doesn't go to the
witness's veracity for truth

00:08:54.600 --> 00:08:58.740
and it doesn't go towards
the Evil Queen's guilt.

00:08:58.740 --> 00:08:59.610
- Exactly.

00:08:59.610 --> 00:09:02.261
- And it's also super gross.
- Yeah, very gross.

00:09:02.261 --> 00:09:03.094
- Help!

00:09:03.094 --> 00:09:03.946
- Order, order!

00:09:03.946 --> 00:09:05.700
Now, are you going somewhere
with this, counselor?

00:09:05.700 --> 00:09:06.533
- No, not really.

00:09:06.533 --> 00:09:08.280
I just wanted to upset
the witness, Your Honor.

00:09:08.280 --> 00:09:09.113
- Looks like you did it.

00:09:09.113 --> 00:09:10.205
- I did it, yeah.

00:09:10.205 --> 00:09:11.510
- If there are no-

00:09:11.510 --> 00:09:12.750
- I don't think a judge, first of all,

00:09:12.750 --> 00:09:14.820
if a lawyer admits that
that was their purpose,

00:09:14.820 --> 00:09:17.880
they're probably gonna be
in some sanction trouble,

00:09:17.880 --> 00:09:18.713
I would imagine.

00:09:18.713 --> 00:09:19.950
- Yeah.

00:09:19.950 --> 00:09:22.140
- Most lawyers would at
least try to play it off

00:09:22.140 --> 00:09:24.810
like they weren't
intentionally trying to do it.

00:09:24.810 --> 00:09:26.940
- Yeah, do not admit that you asked

00:09:26.940 --> 00:09:28.322
a whole bunch of questions in bad faith.

00:09:28.322 --> 00:09:29.155
- Yeah.

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:32.130
Now, that's not to say
that lawyers don't do this

00:09:32.130 --> 00:09:33.930
and try to get under
the skin of witnesses.

00:09:33.930 --> 00:09:36.420
It's definitely a tactic that is done,

00:09:36.420 --> 00:09:38.517
but they're certainly
never gonna admit it.

00:09:38.517 --> 00:09:39.630
You didn't hear it from me.

00:09:39.630 --> 00:09:40.710
- Yeah, not a good lawyer.

00:09:40.710 --> 00:09:42.120
- If there are no further questions,

00:09:42.120 --> 00:09:43.860
it's time for the jury to deliberate.

00:09:43.860 --> 00:09:44.970
- All right, then.

00:09:44.970 --> 00:09:47.160
I guess I ask for a mistrial?

00:09:47.160 --> 00:09:49.560
It's impossible for my
client, the Evil Queen,

00:09:49.560 --> 00:09:50.610
to get a fair trial here,

00:09:50.610 --> 00:09:53.160
because everyone knows
the story of Snow White.

00:09:53.160 --> 00:09:56.070
- Huh, no one has ever
asked for a mistrial.

00:09:56.070 --> 00:09:59.220
- It's an okay argument,
but if you're going, really,

00:09:59.220 --> 00:10:01.170
what she's doing is not
asking for a mistrial

00:10:01.170 --> 00:10:03.330
so much as a motion for change of venue,

00:10:03.330 --> 00:10:05.100
and she really should
have made that motion

00:10:05.100 --> 00:10:07.800
before the court proceeded.

00:10:07.800 --> 00:10:10.680
- Although I do wonder, like,
what venue can they go to

00:10:10.680 --> 00:10:13.020
where they don't know
the story of Snow White?

00:10:13.020 --> 00:10:14.790
- You get a limiting
instruction from the judge

00:10:14.790 --> 00:10:16.710
that says don't refer to
her as the Evil Queen.

00:10:16.710 --> 00:10:18.960
- Yeah, that should definitely
have been done, for sure.

00:10:18.960 --> 00:10:21.420
Yeah, an instruction would be necessary,

00:10:21.420 --> 00:10:23.460
and throughout the whole case,

00:10:23.460 --> 00:10:25.140
just referred to as the Queen.

00:10:25.140 --> 00:10:27.831
- Yeah, or maybe her first name.

00:10:27.831 --> 00:10:29.700
- Her first name is Evil.

00:10:29.700 --> 00:10:31.740
- Instead of implying
royalty or something.

00:10:31.740 --> 00:10:33.210
- So you'll grant me one?

00:10:33.210 --> 00:10:35.460
- Nope, the jury seems
impartial and capable.

00:10:35.460 --> 00:10:37.470
Jury, how do you find the defendant?

00:10:37.470 --> 00:10:40.320
- The impartial, capable,
mysterious, and glamorous jury

00:10:40.320 --> 00:10:42.630
rules in favor of the prosecution.

00:10:42.630 --> 00:10:44.160
We find the Evil Queen guilty.

00:10:44.160 --> 00:10:45.030
- Shocking.

00:10:45.030 --> 00:10:46.620
- Sweet lady justice.

00:10:46.620 --> 00:10:49.170
- Yeah, so I don't think Snow White

00:10:49.170 --> 00:10:51.360
would be a particularly
good piece of literature

00:10:51.360 --> 00:10:52.320
to do a mock trial over,

00:10:52.320 --> 00:10:54.660
because it's so stacked
against the defense.

00:10:54.660 --> 00:10:56.670
- Yeah, well, that's what's
happening here, right?

00:10:56.670 --> 00:11:00.870
- Yeah, I mean what defense
could you come up with

00:11:00.870 --> 00:11:02.070
for the Evil Queen here?

00:11:02.070 --> 00:11:03.370
She poisons Snow White.

00:11:03.370 --> 00:11:05.070
- Could do an insanity defense,

00:11:05.070 --> 00:11:07.020
she didn't know the difference
between right and wrong?

00:11:07.020 --> 00:11:07.853
- Maybe.

00:11:07.853 --> 00:11:08.790
I mean, she's-

00:11:08.790 --> 00:11:11.100
- Although she thinks
she's like mirror, mirror-

00:11:11.100 --> 00:11:12.694
- [Devin] She is talking
to a mirror, though.

00:11:12.694 --> 00:11:14.160
- Maybe she is a little crazy.

00:11:14.160 --> 00:11:15.960
- Yeah, she claims to have a magic mirror

00:11:15.960 --> 00:11:18.570
that can tell her who's the
fairest in all the land.

00:11:18.570 --> 00:11:20.550
You'd have a really tough
time coming up with a defense

00:11:20.550 --> 00:11:21.600
for the Evil Queen here.

00:11:21.600 --> 00:11:22.433
- Yeah.

00:11:24.637 --> 00:11:25.784
- DBD.

00:11:25.784 --> 00:11:26.807
- I don't know.

00:11:26.807 --> 00:11:30.082
I have to sleep on that one.
- Yeah.

00:11:30.082 --> 00:11:31.470
No, I see what you did there.

00:11:31.470 --> 00:11:33.090
- No, I didn't.

00:11:33.090 --> 00:11:34.236
- Yes, you did!

00:11:34.236 --> 00:11:35.310
- I did not!

00:11:35.310 --> 00:11:36.630
- What is going on?

00:11:36.630 --> 00:11:38.640
- Mr. Frond stole my yogurt.

00:11:38.640 --> 00:11:40.620
Look, this is his trash can.

00:11:40.620 --> 00:11:41.730
Gasp, right?

00:11:41.730 --> 00:11:43.500
- I did not eat that yogurt.

00:11:43.500 --> 00:11:45.330
- It's a crime of passion fruit.

00:11:45.330 --> 00:11:46.740
- Sounds like you need a lawyer.

00:11:46.740 --> 00:11:48.690
Hey, let's try this in student court.

00:11:48.690 --> 00:11:49.860
We've already got a judge.

00:11:49.860 --> 00:11:51.270
- Sounds like you need a lawyer.

00:11:51.270 --> 00:11:54.210
I know, at least in the
personal injury area,

00:11:54.210 --> 00:11:55.560
you know, if someone gets in an accident,

00:11:55.560 --> 00:11:56.910
you can't just run up and be like,

00:11:56.910 --> 00:11:58.920
sounds like you need a lawyer.

00:11:58.920 --> 00:12:01.110
I presume it's the same kind of thing,

00:12:01.110 --> 00:12:03.120
like if someone's dealing
with a legal issue,

00:12:03.120 --> 00:12:06.120
lawyers can't just appear
outta nowhere and be,

00:12:06.120 --> 00:12:07.170
hey, here's my card.

00:12:07.170 --> 00:12:08.790
- [Devin] Yeah, yeah, you'd think so.

00:12:08.790 --> 00:12:10.650
- I'm fine taking this to student court.

00:12:10.650 --> 00:12:12.300
Let the whole school see that Mr. Frond

00:12:12.300 --> 00:12:13.770
is a dirty yogurt thief.

00:12:13.770 --> 00:12:15.720
- I am not a thief!

00:12:15.720 --> 00:12:17.700
I'd be happy to take
this to student court.

00:12:17.700 --> 00:12:19.590
- What happens when you find Frond guilty?

00:12:19.590 --> 00:12:21.450
Does this school have the death penalty?

00:12:21.450 --> 00:12:24.360
